{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Creating a Gantt chart: Step 3 and 4"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to describe Gantt charts."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Learning Outcome"}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Assessment Criteria Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through grammar and vocabulary structures.  Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "apt"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "capable"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It's important to select an apt software or template for your Gantt chart."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "underway"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "that is in process of completion"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can check how the project evolves, and make changes as soon as you need, even if the project is in underway."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mess around"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to explore or change something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can also mess around with the format, and the color."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "pivotal"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "that is key, crucial"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Don't forget that you need some pivotal information to complete the diagram."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "marshal"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "in this case, to organize something systematically "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can use numbers or letters to marshal your tasks."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Infinitives of Purpose and Simple Present Tense with General and Specific Determiners"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p><strong>Specific determiners</strong> are words that accompany definite nouns; on the other hand, <strong>general determiners</strong> are words that refer to unspecific people or things.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Infinitives of Purpose </strong></p> <p>Remember that we use the infinitives of purpose to talk about the intention of doing something. The structure that we follow is:</p> <p><strong>Subject + verb + to </strong>+ verb (infinitive) + complement.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>My partners <strong>want me to complete</strong> the Gantt chart for Monday.</i></p> <p>Notice that we can use the <strong>infinitives of purpose</strong> and the verbs with infinitives in the simple present to emphasize the action we want, have or need to do.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>We <strong>need to</strong> relate the tasks and decide which one comes after the other.</i></p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Specific Determiners</strong></p> <p><strong>Specific determiners</strong> are words that accompany definite nouns; this means that the listener knows exactly what you are talking about.&nbsp;</p> <p>Specific determiners are&nbsp;The article <strong>&ldquo;the&rdquo;</strong>, the demonstratives <strong>this, that, these, those, </strong>and the possessives <strong>my, your, his, her, its, our, </strong>or <strong>their.</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>You need to organize <strong>the</strong> chart with <strong>your</strong> tasks.</i></p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>General Determiners</strong></p> <p><strong>General determiners</strong> are words that refer to unspecific people or things, they are&nbsp;<strong>a, an, any, another, other</strong>.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>We need to finish <strong>a</strong> task before beginning&nbsp;<strong>another</strong> activity. </i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You just need to have the information required to create the diagram."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It's important to select an apt software or template for your Gantt chart. These both can help you a lot with the necessary information for your chart."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You can use numbers or letters to marshal your tasks."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Research about different software to create Gantt charts."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Design a comparative chart describing the differences of those software."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your job with your instructor and colleagues."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find more information about software."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a comparative chart describing the characteristics of the software."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I show my chart to my instructor and colleagues."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Types of Charts: Composition Charts"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p><strong>Compositions charts</strong> help you show the components of your information.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>The most common composition chart is the <strong>pie chart</strong>, which allows your viewers to visualize the components of your information and the percentages each variable occupies.</p> <p>On the other hand,<strong> doughnut charts </strong>are also a different design to dispose the information; they are similar to the pie charts. Check the following illustrations to see how these charts look like: </p>"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10img01.jpg"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob10img02.jpg"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le01ob10re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p>Now it&rsquo;s time to create your Gantt chart! You can draw it by hand or you can use any type of software such as Easy projects or Celoxis that offer their services in different languages. Gantto, Matchware, RationalPlan or TeamHeadquarters are well-known Microsoft Project alternatives. Those software applications are easy to use, you just need to have the information required to create the diagram.</p>"}, {"time": "26000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p>Some of these tools are cloud-based, this means different participants can see and edit the chart simultaneously from different locations. It's important to select an apt software or template for your Gantt chart, these both can help you a lot with the necessary information for your chart.</p>\n<p>With some tools specially designed for Microsoft Excel and others, you can have a line showing the everyday progress of a project; you can check how the project evolves, and make changes as soon as you need, even if the project is underway. You can also mess around with the format, and the color, if you want it in 2D or 3D.</p>"}, {"time": "64000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p>Don&rsquo;t forget that you need some pivotal information to complete the diagram. You can draw a plan of your diagram first, schedule all your tasks, and then record it in a program to share it with the stakeholders.</p>\n<p>The main information your chart must have is the tasks that order your project, you can use numbers or letters to marshal your tasks. On the next column, add the start date and then the time to complete each task. Only with this information, you can create a Gantt chart to structuralize your project processes.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le01ob10re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can identify the type of software that I require to create a Gantt chart."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the chart progress in a Gantt chart."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can complete a Gantt diagram with all the required information."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What kind of software tools to create charts do you know?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why do you think it is important to check the progress of a Gantt chart?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_te